claude <claude@p7x.dev> writes:

> Mail delivered into the maildir lands in the working copy alongside
> flag renames and edits, and each new message is committed on its own
> by hand as "mail: <subject>". Copying subjects by hand is slow and
> lets stray characters slip into the log.
>
> commit-mail takes every file added under maildir/ in @ and commits it
> alone, named for its Subject header, so a batch of new mail becomes
> one reviewable commit per message. Renamed and edited files stay in
> the working copy, since a flag change or an archive move is not a new
> message. The prefix defaults to the "mail: " every message commit
> carries today and can be given as an argument:
>
>     just commit-mail 'just: '
> ---
> This applies to the mail branch, not trunk; base-commit is mail@upstream.
> Encoded (RFC 2047) subjects are kept as written; none are on the list
> yet.
>
> change-id: ppvulnkkwnrpxzusskntwqrloyrlnllu
>
>  Justfile | 17 +++++++++++++++++
>  1 file changed, 17 insertions(+)
>
> diff --git a/Justfile b/Justfile
> index 7945f87..7e65109 100644
> --- a/Justfile
> +++ b/Justfile
> @@ -19,3 +19,20 @@ push:
>    jj git push -b mail --quiet --remote jails
>    jj git push -b mail --quiet --remote github
>    jj git export
> +
> +# commit each mail file added in @ on its own, named for its subject
> +commit-mail $prefix="mail: ":
> +  #!/bin/sh
> +  set -eu
> +  added="$(jj diff -r @ -T 'if(self.status() == "added", self.path() ++ "\n")' 'root:maildir')"
> +  printf '%s\n' "${added}" | while IFS= read -r f; do
> +    [ -n "${f}" ] || continue
> +    subject="$(awk '
> +      /^\r?$/ { exit }
> +      insub && /^[ \t]/ { sub(/^[ \t]+/, " "); s = s $0; next }
> +      insub { exit }
> +      tolower($0) ~ /^subject:/ { sub(/^[^:]*:[ \t]*/, ""); s = $0; insub = 1 }
> +      END { sub(/\r$/, "", s); print s }
> +    ' "${f}")"
> +    jj commit -m "${prefix}${subject:-(no subject)}" "root-file:\"${f}\""
> +  done
>
> base-commit: dd1db9aa2c310f456800269c0c8104f1a8a8fedf
